---
// src/components/JuliusHeadenHistory.astro
---

<section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 bg-white text-slate-800" id="lodge-history">
  <div class="mb-16 text-center">
    <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">The Legacy of Julius A. Headen</h2>
    <div class="w-24 h-1.5 bg-blue-900 mx-auto mt-6 rounded-full"></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-12 lg:gap-16 items-start">
    
    <!-- Left Column: Biography & Core Attributes -->
    <div class="space-y-8 text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <div class="space-y-6 text-slate-700">
        <p>
          Julius A.
           Headen was a native of North Carolina, a United States Army veteran, and a skilled builder who made his home in Washington state with his wife, Antonia Headen. Remembered as a pillar of the Tacoma community, he was a dedicated mentor who guided young men during a time when Masonry was experiencing a generational shift.
        </p>
        <p>
          Headen emphasized leadership rooted in integrity, fairness, and the foundational principles of the order: the Holy Bible, the square, and the compass. He consistently prioritized the success of the lodge, sacrificing personal time and resources to ensure its prosperity.
        </p>
      </div>
      
      <div class="bg-slate-50 p-8 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6 border-b border-slate-200 pb-3">Core Attributes</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-5">
          <li class="flex flex-col">
            <span class="font-bold text-blue-900 text-xl">Mentorship</span>
            <span class="text-slate-600 mt-1">Bridged the generation gap by setting high ideals and a lasting precedent for young men to follow.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-col">
            <span class="font-bold text-blue-900 text-xl">Community Leader</span>
            <span class="text-slate-600 mt-1">Recognized across Tacoma as a skilled physical mason, kind guide, and respected civic presence.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-col">
            <span class="font-bold text-blue-900 text-xl">Dedication</span>
            <span class="text-slate-600 mt-1">Routinely sacrificed personal time and finances to uphold and advance the Masonic order.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Right Column: Leadership & Timeline -->
    <div class="space-y-12 text-lg">
      
      <!-- Masonic Leadership -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900">Masonic Leadership</h3>
        <p class="text-slate-700">
          Before the formation of his namesake lodge, Brother Headen was a fiercely dedicated member of <strong class="text-slate-900">Cassia Lodge No. 5</strong> in Tacoma, Washington. His professionalism and work ethic earned him the following leadership roles:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-700 marker:text-blue-900">
          <li><strong class="text-slate-900">Worshipful Master:</strong> Elected to lead Cassia No. 5 in 1974.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-slate-900">Grand Lodge Trustee:</strong> Served the jurisdiction for 13 consecutive years.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-6 italic text-slate-500 border-l-4 border-slate-300 pl-4">
          In February 1993, Brother Headen passed away and was laid to rest at the New Tacoma Cemetery in Tacoma, Washington.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Timeline -->
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-8">The Founding of Lodge No. 102</h3>
        
        <div class="relative border-l-2 border-blue-900/30 ml-4 space-y-10">
          
          <div class="relative pl-8">
            <div class="absolute w-4 h-4 bg-blue-900 rounded-full -left-[9px] top-1.5 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-slate-900">1993: Grand Lodge Initiative</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mt-2">The Grand Lodge of Washington establishes the objective to form a Prince Hall Lodge in Federal Way. Brother Lonnie Traylor is assigned the mission.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="relative pl-8">
            <div class="absolute w-4 h-4 bg-blue-900 rounded-full -left-[9px] top-1.5 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-slate-900">March 1994: Study Club Formed</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mt-2">The Julius A. Headen study club begins meeting at the old hall of Thornton F. McElroy Lodge. The MWGM issues a dispensation to operate under the MWPHGL of WA.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="relative pl-8">
            <div class="absolute w-4 h-4 bg-blue-900 rounded-full -left-[9px] top-1.5 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-slate-900">July 1995: Charter Issued</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mt-2">During the Annual Communication, Julius A. Headen U.D. is officially presented with Charter No. 102.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="relative pl-8">
            <div class="absolute w-4 h-4 bg-blue-900 rounded-full -left-[9px] top-1.5 ring-4 ring-white"></div>
            <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-slate-900">September 1995: Lodge Consecration</h4>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mt-2">Grand Lodge Officers consecrate Julius A. Headen Lodge No. 102 to operate under the jurisdiction of the MWPHGL of WA, upholding the principles of Friendship, Morality, and Brotherly Love.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>